# DESIGN DECISION: ggdef/language divergence on Set positional index. ggdef
# eval.rs:944-948 accepts + returns insertion-ordered element; Rust/SH
# silently accept and return garbage (already-filed
# set_index_returns_garbage.gg); filed intent is REJECT. Core #8 gate — ≥1
# bug in one of the two. Track E filing 2026-08-02 owner-visible.
#
# WRONG TODAY (measured at HEAD): three lanes disagree on `s[0]` over a Set:
#   - Rust gg (C+LLVM): silently accepts, prints a raw address (garbage).
#   - Self-host (SH): mirrors Rust gg (silent-accept, garbage).
#   - ggdef spec (`spec/ggdef/src/eval.rs:944-948`): accepts and returns the
#     insertion-ordered element (well-defined behaviour).
#
# INTENDED per language design intent (see `set_index_returns_garbage.gg`):
# a check-time REJECTION. A Set has no positional index in Gorget, so `s[0]`
# should never accept. That makes ggdef's current well-defined semantics a
# SPEC DEFECT (adopt-and-reject the spec's positional accept), OR — if the
# owner reverses the language intent — Rust gg + SH silently returning
# garbage becomes the ≥1 real bug on each lane (Core #8).
#
# DESIGN DECISION owed: owner picks (a) keep intent = REJECT (spec fix on
# ggdef) OR (b) accept + return insertion-order like ggdef (Rust/SH fix).
# The fixture asserts the REJECT (per filed intent); flip when the owner
# rules.
#
# Set REQUIRES `@derive(Equatable, Hashable)` on the struct payload.
@derive(Equatable, Hashable)
struct P:
    int x

int main():
    Set[P] s = {}
    s.add(P(1))
    s.add(P(2))
    P p = s[0]
    print(f"{p.x}")
    return 0
